  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAs the remnants of Isaias continue on a northeast trajectory, \nwe will see any lingering drizzle in our eastern counties come\nto an end early this morning and clearing skies through the \nday. With full sunshine this afternoon, high temperatures will \nclimb right back up to well above normal readings from the upper\n70s east to the middle 80s to around 90 degrees west. Mostly \nsunny to sunny skies will continue into Monday and Tuesday, \nalong with breezy southwest winds. This will result in near \nrecord to record highs from around 90 degrees to the middle \n90s.\n\nOn Tuesday, models continue to show a frontal boundary sinking south \ntoward our region, moving into the PAH forecast area (fa) Wednesday. \nThe model blend brings 20-30% chances of showers and thunderstorms \ninto north and northwest portions of the PAH fa late Tuesday night, \nwith chances increasing and spreading southeast across our\nentire area Wednesday. 30-70% chances, best chances north and \nwest, persist from Wednesday afternoon into Thursday as the \nfrontal boundary lingers across the middle Mississippi and lower\nOhio valleys. The boundary finally moves on through Thursday\nnight into Friday, with chances of showers and a few storms\ndwindling to 20-30% Thursday night, and just 20% chances of some\nshowers lingering on Friday.\n\nClouds and precipitation will bring down temperatures a little\nWednesday and Thursday, but readings will remain well above\nnormal. Behind the frontal boundary Friday temperatures will\ncome down to seasonable values. By Saturday, under the \ninfluence of northwest winds, temperatures could be as much as\n10 degrees below normal.",
